WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.728 --> 00:00:05.035
- Thirty-five percent of Americans between 18 and 45 have
   
2
00:00:05,035 --&gt; 00:00:07,868
at least one tattoo on their body.

00:00:10.688 --> 00:00:13.376
Well upon your death,
those tattoos are gone,

00:00:13.376 --> 00:00:15.712
they're only permanent
as long as you're alive

00:00:15.712 --> 00:00:18.062
but then they're gone.

00:00:18.062 --> 00:00:20.145
We've changed that world.

00:00:22.979 --> 00:00:25.453
- [Narrator] So all of
these tattoos used to be

00:00:25.453 --> 00:00:26.755
on someone's body.

00:00:26.755 --> 00:00:29.505
- [Artist] Yes, everyone of them.

00:00:35.950 --> 00:00:39.867
- [Narrator] Charles Hamm
has a lot of tattoos.

00:00:42.168 --> 00:00:44.440
- I'm the Executive Director
and Chairman of the Board

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:47.023
of the National Association
for the Preservation

00:00:47.023 --> 00:00:48.730
of Skin Art.

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:50.147
We call it NAPSA.

00:00:51.247 --> 00:00:52.773
You know, I got all these beautiful tattoos

00:00:52.773 --> 00:00:54.735
and I'm going to get cremated when I die,

00:00:54.735 --> 00:00:56.122
and everything goes up in smoke.

00:00:56.122 --> 00:00:58.789
So all the meaning, all
the things that people know

00:00:58.789 --> 00:01:02.706
about me that are close
to me are gone as well.

00:01:07.397 --> 00:01:09.189
- So Charles, which tattoo do I get?

00:01:09.189 --> 00:01:10.191
- I can't tell you buddy.

00:01:10.191 --> 00:01:13.029
Most of my upper body's claimed.

00:01:13.029 --> 00:01:14.735
- [Narrator] So, why would anyone want

00:01:14.735 --> 00:01:17.925
their tattoos cut off and hung on a wall

00:01:17.925 --> 00:01:19.092
for posterity?

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:25.253
This is Shelly Krajny.

00:01:25.253 --> 00:01:28.389
She's one of NAPSA's first members.

00:01:28.389 --> 00:01:32.107
- I was really nervous, um, when
I first got my tattoo done

00:01:32.107 --> 00:01:33.346
to show my mom.

00:01:33.346 --> 00:01:36.002
The first thing that she said
when she saw it was that, like,

00:01:36.002 --> 00:01:39.106
"It's so beautiful, I wish
that I could like hang

00:01:39.106 --> 00:01:40.606
that on my wall."

00:01:41.783 --> 00:01:44.162
This is on my body forever.

00:01:44.162 --> 00:01:47.959
Now, it can be part of
someone else's story forever

00:01:47.959 --> 00:01:49.292
thanks to NAPSA.

00:01:50.871 --> 00:01:52.482
- [Narrator] Here's how it all works.

00:01:52.482 --> 00:01:54.604
Within hours of a person's death,

00:01:54.604 --> 00:01:56.484
NAPSA will mail a preservation kit

00:01:56.484 --> 00:01:57.772
to their family.

00:01:57.772 --> 00:01:59.479
The mortician will then cut off

00:01:59.479 --> 00:02:02.231
the tattooed skin and apply NAPSA's

00:02:02.231 --> 00:02:04.023
embalming solution.

00:02:04.023 --> 00:02:06.946
The tattoo is then mailed
to NAPSA's headquarters

00:02:06.946 --> 00:02:09.207
inside this office complex just outside

00:02:09.207 --> 00:02:11.084
of Cleveland, Ohio.

00:02:11.084 --> 00:02:13.772
This is where they finish
processing the tattoo.

00:02:13.772 --> 00:02:15.650
It's no longer skin now.

00:02:15.650 --> 00:02:18.042
It has the look of leather but it feels

00:02:18.042 --> 00:02:20.186
like parchment paper.

00:02:20.186 --> 00:02:22.639
Finally, NAPSA sends the framed tattoo

00:02:22.639 --> 00:02:24.222
to the beneficiary.

00:02:25.733 --> 00:02:27.866
- Everybody thought I was
crazy at first in my family

00:02:27.866 --> 00:02:31.151
but they quickly came to realize
this is a beautiful thing,

00:02:31.151 --> 00:02:34.575
and they've claimed their tattoos.

00:02:34.575 --> 00:02:37.146
I have a gorilla on my chest.

00:02:37.146 --> 00:02:38.917
And gorillas are known for protecting

00:02:38.917 --> 00:02:41.253
all their females in their group,

00:02:41.253 --> 00:02:44.420
and my wife's name resides under that.

00:02:46.982 --> 00:02:49.541
That's my way of saying
that I'm protecting my wife.

00:02:49.541 --> 00:02:51.365
This gorilla's overlooking her.

00:02:51.365 --> 00:02:53.349
So upon my passing, that put in a frame,

00:02:53.349 --> 00:02:56.023
sitting above a fireplace,
will remind her of me

00:02:56.023 --> 00:02:57.953
every time that she looks at it.

00:02:57.953 --> 00:03:01.036
And that's really what this is about.

